# c3Ford — Odhrán at the ford
#
#   node tools/dialogue/compile.mjs tools/dialogue/drafts/c3Ford.dlg --check
#   node tools/dialogue/compile.mjs tools/dialogue/drafts/c3Ford.dlg
#
# ── WHAT THIS IS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The toll-man Muireann warns about at d3Sea node 4. Cut down hard from the
# prose draft: that ran ~50 lines, which at staged reveal and syllable-timed
# pacing is 15+ cards on the mainline. Here the mainline is FOUR cards --
# arrive, learn there is no toll today, ask one thing, leave -- and
# everything else is dug for. Each layer costs an option and sets a note
# that opens the next node.
#
#   node 0   the rope is down. He does not get up.
#   node 1   he cannot reckon the toll            (asked_odhran_why)
#   node 2   the feast, the bard, the satire      (knows_odhran_shame)
#   node 3   the binding, the cave, the rope      (knows_odhran_binding)
#   node 4   afterwards
#
# ── THE BYPASS IS THE TRAP ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# has_druid_word (set at d3Sea node 4) puts a one-tap exit on node 0. Use it
# and you are across in two cards, free, and you never learn any of this --
# which is exactly what Muireann told you to do: "You need say no more than
# that. And say no more." The efficient player loses the scene and never
# finds out. It costs nothing to leave in, and it is the first branch the
# player can feel they chose.
#
# It is only offered @first, so it does not sit there nagging on later
# visits once the conversation has been opened.
#
# ── OPEN QUESTIONS FOR RIBO ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   @file  is a GUESS. The river runs a3/b3/c3/d3 and c3 sits between the
#          estuary and the turn north to the ráth, so the ford is probably
#          there. Change it if the ford lives elsewhere.
#
#   NAMES  Muireann puts the ráth on Fionnbarra's land and Odhrán is
#          Fionnbarra's man -- but the chapter arc has Ruadhán mac Colla as
#          the chief being extorted by Séadna. One man, two names, or two
#          chiefs? I have used Fionnbarra throughout to match d3Sea, and
#          named the bard Séadna at node 2, which is the point where the
#          player could connect him to the ráth. Both are one-word changes.
#
#   RIDDLE No answer is required here. The arithmetic riddle is spoken at
#          node 3 and left standing. If you do want the division-by-zero
#          solve later, the hook is that he cannot name one honest man --
#          the Éasca input would slot in as an option on node 3 and set a
#          note. Nothing here depends on it either way.
#
#   Cnoc Maol, not Croc Mael.
#
# ── REGISTER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Odhrán is plain throughout -- the thou/thee of the prose draft read
# Malory rather than saga. The heightened voice belongs only to the satire
# itself at node 2, because that is verse and quoted, and the contrast is
# the point: the bard's words are the only ornamented thing in the scene,
# and they are what broke him.
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


@file       c3Ford
@encounter  0


= node 0 — the rope is down
@when !note met_odhran
@note met_odhran
@again The rope is down. Go across. / 
He does not get up.
% 
The rope is down. The ford is open.
% 
Go across, if you are going.
% 

* Say the druid's word
% 
@when note has_druid_word
@first
@exit
@note used_druid_word
> The Druid of the Cliff-Mouth knows my name.
% 
< Then he knows more than I do these days.
% 
< Go on across. There is no toll on you.
% 

* What toll is this place keeping?
% 
> What toll is this place keeping?
% 
< That is the question, right enough.
% 
< I have been at it since the moon turned and I am no nearer.
% 

* Are you hurt?
% 
@note asked_wound
> Are you hurt, man? Have you taken a wound?
% 
< A wound. That is the word for it.
% 
< I was struck, and struck hard, and there is no blood on me at all.
% 
< That is the worst of it. There is nothing to show.
% 

* Walk on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 1 — he cannot reckon
@hold
@again Well? / 
Name your toll and be done, you are thinking.
% 
I cannot name it.
% 

* You cannot name your own toll?
% 
@first
@note asked_odhran_why
> You are a toll-keeper at a ford and you cannot name a toll?
% 
< I was Odhrán, toll-keeper to Fionnbarra of the three rivers.
% 
< I held this ford and the three crossings north to Druim Caillí.
% 
< I took his portion from merchant and pilgrim and swineherd alike,
% 
< and I was never once bested at it. Ask anyone on this water.
% 

* What is it you are counting?
% 
@first
> What is it you are counting, then?
% 
< Fourteen ox-loads of salt fish, and half again of what is left
% 
< after the church takes its tithe, against the weight of a hull
% 
< in dry timber and the same hull in green.
% 
< Every road I take through it comes out at a place that cannot be.
% 

* I have seen tribute reckoned before
% 
@first
> I have seen tribute reckoned, and hostage-prices, and silver weighed.
% 
> None of them wore this shape.
% 
< None of them were made in shame.
% 

* Leave him to it
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 2 — the feast
@when note asked_odhran_why
@hold
@again It was very well made. / 
I went up to the hall as a man of position goes.
% 
My tally-rod, my good wool cloak, my belt of tooled leather.
% 
I sat near the fire, which is a good place, with meat on my bone.
% 
The chief said my name aloud before his warriors. That is no small thing.
% 

* What happened at the hall?
% 
@first
@note knows_odhran_shame
> And what happened to you there?
% 
< There was a bard. A lean one, sharp in the mouth, black clever eyes.
% 
< Séadna, they called him. He made a satire.
% 
< Upon me.
% 

* What did he say?
% 
@when note knows_odhran_shame
@first
> What did he say of you?
% 
< That my heart was a stone in a locked chest, and the chest sunk in a bog.
% 
< That Fionnbarra's hound took meat from a hand with more grace
% 
< than I took toll. That I had grown so round from the taking of it
% 
< that sitting on my rock a man could not tell which was which,
% 
< except that the rock did not smack its lips.
% 
< He rhymed it all. It was very well made. That is the thing of it.
% 

* Was there more?
% 
@when note knows_odhran_shame
@first
@note knows_odhran_binding
> Was that the end of it?
% 
< He said I was known from Dún Bolg to the northern stones
% 
< as the man who took the last dry heel of bread out of a widow's basket
% 
< and weighed it before he gave her back the half.
% 
< And then he bound a reckoning into it, and that is what will not come out.
% 

* Leave him to it
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 3 — the binding
@when note knows_odhran_binding
@hold
@again I cannot find the end of it. / 
A bard's scorn, when he means it, is not insult.
% 
It is a thing with weight, and it does what it says.
% 

* What did he bind on you?
% 
@first
> What was it he bound on you?
% 
< He spoke it before all of them, and it went thus:
% 
< "No peace to you, Odhrán, no quiet night, no easy morning,
% 
< no still hour between one bell and the next, until you reckon this:
% 
< take the number of your years; divide it by the number of honest men
% 
< you have known in all your life; multiply what is left
% 
< by the number of times you were glad of your own company.
% 
< Speak that sum aloud at your own ford, and you are free."
% 

* Have you brought it to anyone?
% 
@first
> Has no one been able to work it?
% 
< The monks at the black abbey turned it over and said
% 
< it was not their manner of reckoning.
% 
< The old woman on the hill who counts in knots laughed till she wept.
% 
< Fionnbarra sent three wise men to this bridge. Three went away again
% 
< rubbing their beards and saying nothing of any use.
% 

* What will you do?
% 
@first
@note knows_odhran_cave
> And what will you do, if it will not come free?
% 
< There is a cave above Cnoc Maol where a man could sit very quietly
% 
< and not take toll off anyone ever again.
% 
< The monks go into caves. They sit in the dark and reckon nothing
% 
< and nobody mocks them for it. It is thought holy.
% 

* Ask for the rope
% 
@when note knows_odhran_cave
@first
@note has_toll_rope
> I have a long road west. I could use a rope.
% 
< Take it. Take it freely, there is no toll on you.
% 
< It goes across the ford when there is traffic and comes up when there is none.
% 
< It is a good rope. It was always a good rope.
% 

* Leave him to it
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 4 — afterwards
@hold
@again The ford is open. / 
The ford is open. It costs you nothing.
% 

* Is there any change in it?
% 
> Is there any change in the reckoning?
% 
< None. But it is a thing that can be carried, I find,
% 
< so long as somebody knows it is there.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent
